# src/index.njk for the reasoning. Every sentence sits in a {% t %} block and
# renders untouched on the English page.
pagination:
  data: languages
  size: 1
  alias: language
  addAllPagesToCollections: true
permalink: "{{ language.prefix }}/who-to-pitch/"
---

{#- #153, 24 Aug 2026. The first half of the product thesis, given a page of
    its own because the header used to point at an anchor on the homepage.
    Everything claimed here is something the studio actually does — the run's
    identity phase, fit scoring, the confidence rating, Beast Mode, warm paths.
    If a claim on this page stops being true, this page is wrong, not modest. -#}

<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}the first question{% endt %}</span>
    <h1 class="page-h1">{% t %}Who to pitch{% endt %}</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      {% t %}A list of companies is easy to get and nearly worthless. What you need is a
      short list you could defend out loud — each one with the case for it, the
      case against it, a real person to write to, and the shortest warm path in.
      Customers, distribution partners, resellers, companies worth investing in:
      whichever of those you asked for. That is what a run gives you before it
      writes a word.{% endt %}
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-actions">
      <a class="btn-cta" href="{{ '/#scan' | localize }}">{% t %}Scan your site — free{% endt %}</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="{{ '/what-to-send/' | localize }}">{% t %}Then: what to send →{% endt %}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="flow">
  <div class="flow-inner">

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">01</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}It works out who the company actually is — before anything else runs.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}A target is identified by its website, not by its name. The studio opens
          the site and follows the pages that name an operator — footer, terms,
          privacy, about — reads what they sell and to whom, and only then starts
          searching. Everything downstream is handed that as settled fact.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}It reports back the pages it opened and the same-named businesses it
          <em>ruled out</em>. That second list is the point: "Northwind" is four
          companies, and a pitch researched against the wrong one is worse than no
          pitch at all.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}You see the pages read and the companies ruled out, on every run.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">02</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}It scores fit against your brief — and argues both sides.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Your brief says what you are hunting for, in your own words — new
          customers, agencies who could resell you, companies worth investing in,
          whatever it actually is. Every company found is scored against that, and
          the score comes with a written argument and the signals underneath it —
          the ones for and the ones against, side by side, each rated by how recent
          it is and how often it recurs.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}A tool that only ever says yes is a tool you stop reading. The signals
          against a company are what make a shortlist worth the name.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}Fit thesis · signals for and against · recency and recurrence on each.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">03</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}Decision-makers, with sources. No source, no name.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}A name appears only where a credible public source supports it, and the
          pitch links what was read. Every name is rated — <strong>confirmed</strong>
          when the company or the person states the role, <strong>likely</strong> when
          a secondary source does, <strong>unverified</strong> when it is inference.
          Where nothing backs a name, the role comes back blank for enrichment
          rather than filled with a guess.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}A LinkedIn profile is linked only where one was actually read. Where it
          was not, you get a search instead, drawn dashed, so it can never pass for
          a profile the studio found.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}The confidence rating is about the name, not the person.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">04</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}Lookalikes, so one good target becomes a quarter's worth.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Every run ends with companies like the one you just researched. Tick the
          ones worth pursuing and the button counts them — <em>"Run 3 pitches"</em> —
          so a shortlist goes out in one action. A bracketed name is a gap in the
          research rather than a target, so it never gets a checkbox.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}Already have the list? Paste it. Up to a hundred companies at a time,
          researched and drafted while the tab is closed.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}A run is a background job. Start it and leave — it finishes without you.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">05</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}The warm path in, from the network you already have.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Upload your connections once and every target is checked against them.
          Where somebody you already know can open the door, you get the chain —
          you, the connector, the company — and who they are to you.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}Email addresses in a LinkedIn export are deleted at import, and your
          network never travels on a shared pitch.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}And the intro request comes drafted — that half is on the next page.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="page-split">
  <div class="page-split-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}the honest bit{% endt %}</span>
    <h2>{% t %}This half is free, and it is the easier half.{% endt %}</h2>
    <p>
      {% t %}Point the scan at your own site and it will tell you who it would hunt for
      you, without an account and without a card. We give it away because a list
      of names is not the thing that is hard to get, and it is not the thing you
      are behind on.{% endt %}
    </p>
    <p>
      {% t %}What takes an afternoon per company is the other half — the deck, the
      emails, the proposal and the call sheet, in your voice, with nothing
      invented. That is the half Prospektor is actually for.{% endt %}
    </p>
    <a class="btn-cta" href="{{ '/what-to-send/' | localize }}">{% t %}What to send →{% endt %}</a>
  </div>
</section>
